!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/32/ltr8/http/problem-4.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/32/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/32/m/core.tn"
@doc:"""
  The error body this project writes when a request cannot be fulfilled.

  This is tson-http's own schema, maintained here. It began as a copy of tson-cli's diagnostics.tn
  and has since diverged, which is the right outcome rather than drift: a CLI reports on files and a
  server reports on requests, and holding one shape across both would have meant neither fitting.

  problem follows RFC 9457 (Problem Details for HTTP APIs, obsoleting 7807) so that ordinary HTTP
  tooling recognises the body: type, title, status, detail and instance are its five members, and
  errors is an extension member of exactly the shape §3.1 of that RFC uses for its own
  multiple-validation-errors example.

  diagnostic stays close to what a TSON read produces, because that is what it reports. It is not
  RFC 9457's per-error shape -- that RFC suggests detail and pointer, which would throw away the
  code vocabulary, the two-ended location model and the source positions §8.1 requires.

  Versioned per §10: problem-1.tn, problem-2.tn and problem-3.tn are superseded by this and are all
  still published, because a document that named any of them must go on resolving. A shape change is
  a new name, never an edit -- and adding an enum member is a shape change, which is why each new
  diagnostic code has arrived under a new version rather than by editing the last.
  """
{
  @doc:"""
    A hand-written copy of io.ltr8.tson.compiler.Diagnostic.Code, which is the source of truth;
    TsonProblemSchemaTest is the only thing that checks the two agree.

    Two members are not a verdict on the request, and both are answered 5xx rather than 400.
    NOT_IMPLEMENTED says the library could not check a construct the body uses -- the same split the
    CLI rides its exit codes on (70, not 1). BIND_MISMATCH says a schema and the Java class bound to
    it disagree, which is this server's own wiring; its message names a server class, so it is a 500
    and the boundary drops it from the body entirely.

    They are declared here even though a client should never see either, because this enum copies
    Diagnostic.Code rather than the subset one server happens to emit.
    """
  diagnostic_code => !enum [
    FIELD_REQUIRED FIELD_FIXED TYPE_MISMATCH WRONG_ARITY UNKNOWN_TYPE_REF ATOM_CONSTRAINT_VIOLATION
    UNRECOGNIZED_FIELD DUPLICATE_MAP_KEY DUPLICATE_FIELD SCHEMA_ERROR UNKNOWN_TYPE VALIDATION_ERROR
    NOT_IMPLEMENTED BIND_MISMATCH
  ]
  @doc:"""
    One problem, located at up to two ends: the value in the data (path, data_position) and the rule
    in the schema (schema_id, schema_pointer, schema_position). Either end may be absent -- a problem
    found in a schema has no data, and a schemaless read has no schema.

    A position is "line:column:byte_offset", all three 1-based except byte_offset, which counts
    UTF-8 bytes from 0 ([TSON-DATA] §8.1). The offsets are into the request body as sent.
    """
  diagnostic => {
    path: text?
    schema_pointer: text?
    schema_id: text?
    code: diagnostic_code
    message: text
    expected: text?
    actual: text?
    data_position: text?
    schema_position: text?
  }
  @doc:"""
    RFC 9457's five members, plus errors.

    type identifies the class of failure and is the member to match on: it is stable where title is
    prose, and it dereferences. Absent means RFC 9457's "about:blank" -- no semantics beyond the
    status code. title summarises the type; detail describes this one occurrence. instance
    identifies this occurrence, and may be relative, per that RFC.

    status is restated here so a problem that is stored, logged or forwarded stays self-describing
    once separated from its response.

    errors is empty for a failure that produced no diagnostic at all -- an unsupported media type, an
    unacceptable Accept -- which is why it is a list rather than a required single value.
    """
  problem => {
    type: uri?
    title: text
    status: int32
    detail: text?
    instance: uri?
    errors: [diagnostic]
  }
}
